First, let me think about the Levenshtein distance implementation:

```javascript
function levenshteinDistance(str1, str2) {
  const m = str1.length;
  const n = str2.length;

  // Create a matrix
  const dp = Array.from({ length: m + 1 }, () => Array(n + 1).fill(0));

  // Initialize
  for (let i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;
  for (let j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;

  // Fill in the matrix
  for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
      if (str1[i - 1] === str2[j - 1]) {
        dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
      } else {
        dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(
          dp[i - 1][j],    // deletion
          dp[i][j - 1],    // insertion
          dp[i - 1][j - 1] // substitution
        );
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[m][n];
}
```

For the closest match algorithm:
- For each available property, calculate the Levenshtein distance
- Track properties with distance ≤ 3
- Among those, find the minimum distance
- If multiple tie, return alphabetically first

